{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"antelopine-limivorous-chinchilla","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nThe in-process library read path (`rebar._reads`) has **no initialization guard**, so every\nread against a repo whose tracker directory does not exist returns an EMPTY result with no\nerror. The CLI read path guards this exact condition\n(`_engine_support/reads_cli.py:340-342` -> \"ticket system not initialized\", exit 1), and the\nlibrary WRITE path guards it too (`_store/event_prepare.py:99-102`), but the library/MCP READ\npath does not.\n\nBecause the MCP read tools delegate straight to the library (`_mcp_reads.py:295` ->\n`rebar.list_tickets`), an MCP client pointed at a host with a missing store is told\n\"there are no tickets\" instead of \"this store is not initialized\".\n\n## Impact (observed in production)\n\nThe deployed rebar MCP server at `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp` has no\n`/app/.tickets-tracker`. It reports \"Connected\" and answers:\n\n- `list_tickets` / `search` / `ready_tickets` -> `[]`   (silent)\n- `show_ticket <real alias>`                  -> \"ticket_not_found\"  (loud but WRONG cause)\n- `fsck`                                      -> \"ticket system not initialized (/app/.tickets-tracker not found)\"  (correct)\n\nAn agent following the repo's MCP-first tracker rule therefore sees an empty tracker,\nconcludes no ticket exists for its work, and files duplicates -- the precise\nsilent-data-hiding failure mode already recorded as a defect for the store-divergence case\nin bug bilingual-capitalist-caecilian (\"Silent data-hiding: no error is surfaced to the\noperator; the store simply looks empty\") and for the filter case in bug spiny-ferry-ripen.\n\n## Authoritative sources violated\n\n- `docs/mcp-reference.md:7` -- MCP faults are surfaced as distinct error codes \"instead of\n  silently resolving to a fallback\", so \"a driving agent can tell a broken config from a\n  deliberate policy refusal\". `[]` for a missing store defeats exactly this.\n- Surface parity: the CLI (`reads_cli.py:340`) and library writes (`event_prepare.py:99`)\n  both raise on this condition; library reads alone do not.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`_reads.py` resolves the tracker through `_tracker()` (`_reads.py:31-33`) and never checks\nthat the directory exists; all 8 read entries funnel through it unguarded.\n\nOrigin: commit 7d53bc5b43b7 (2026-06-09, \"#2 Steps 0/2/3\") introduced the in-process read\npath, stating it replicated \"each shim's exact contract\" while explicitly noting the\nin-process reads \"do not run the dispatcher's `_ensure_initialized`\". Its parity test\ncovered an *empty* store but never an *uninitialized* one; that test was later deleted as\n\"now-moot\" by 28406790c61e. Ticket: tend-tomb-doe (df9b-663a-98ed-4dc4), whose own scope was\n\"Map in-process exceptions to existing RebarError shapes\" + \"parity tests green both ways\".\n\n## Reproduction (purely local, no network)\n\n    tmp=$(mktemp -d); cd \"$tmp\"; git init -q .\n    python3 -c \"import rebar; print(rebar.list_tickets(repo_root='$tmp'))\"   # -> []      (silent)\n    python3 -c \"import rebar; rebar.create_ticket('task','x',repo_root='$tmp')\"  # -> RebarError: not initialized\n    rebar list                                                              # -> Error: not initialized, exit 1\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A library read (`list_tickets`, `search`, `recent_session_logs`) against a repo with no\n      tracker directory raises the same not-initialized `RebarError` the write path raises,\n      instead of returning `[]`.\n- [ ] `show_ticket` against an uninitialized store reports the store as uninitialized rather\n      than the misleading \"Ticket '<id>' not found\".\n- [ ] The corresponding MCP read tools surface that fault as a machine-readable error\n      envelope, not an empty array.\n- [ ] An INITIALIZED-but-empty store still returns `[]` with no error (no regression).\n- [ ] Regression coverage asserts read/write parity on an uninitialized store.","id":"44fd-230c-5893-4246","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["store","mcp","reads","data-integrity"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Library and MCP reads mask an uninitialized store as an empty one (list/search/ready return [] instead of erroring)"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787705735137922001,"uuid":"ef689675-8aa8-4d88-b41e-8989f6e5d295"}